//! flags: -p core

// What an `element` and a `match` on one become.
//
// The layout is Rust's repr(C) enum, which is what Lower/ADTLayout.cpp picks
// when nothing says otherwise: a tag, then a union of the variants. Fields
// keep the order they were declared in. The tag is the smallest unsigned type
// that can tell the variants apart, so a sum of two is one byte even though
// the padding after it is decided by the payload's alignment either way.
//
// The variants here are deliberately unequal -- a sphere is a point and a
// radius, a triangle is three points -- so the union has to be as big as its
// largest member rather than all of them being interchangeable.
//
// A `match` becomes a test per arm in the order they were written, and the
// last arm gets no test: exhaustiveness is checked when the match is built, so
// once the others are ruled out it is the only thing left. The arm's bindings
// are reads of the payload at that variant's type, and they are inside the arm
// because reading a field of a variant a value is not would read whatever
// those bytes happen to be.
//
// `Shape_Sph` and `Shape_Tri` are not written below. They are generated,
// one per variant, because `area` is exported and so a caller outside bonsai
// can hold a `Shape` and will need to make one. Building it by hand would mean
// writing this pass's tag numbering into that caller, where nothing would
// catch it drifting; a constructor keeps the numbering here. They are ordinary
// exported functions whose body is a Construct, so they lower through the same
// code as a Construct written by hand and every backend gets them.
//
// This is `-p core`, which stops before the optimizers. `center` is bound and
// unused in the Sph arm; the default pipeline's DCE deletes it, and
// tests/bonsai/correctness/llvm/adt.bonsai is the one that runs the result.
import stdlib/numerics;

element Shape =
    | Sph(center : vec3f, radius : Float)
    | Tri(p0 : vec3f, p1 : vec3f, p2 : vec3f);

// pbrt: Shape::Area(), which every shape answers differently.
func [[export]] area(s : Shape) -> Float {
    match s {
        Sph(center, radius) => return 4.0 * 3.14159265 * radius * radius;
        Tri(p0, p1, p2) => return 0.5 * norm(cross(p1 - p0, p2 - p0));
    }
}

// Building one, which is what a scene description does.
func [[export]] unit_sphere() -> Shape = Sph(vec3f{0.0, 0.0, 0.0}, 1.0);
